what if i told you an 86 year old  hungarian-american psychologist could  help you understand how to feel as happy  as possible from playing games  let me explain  earlier this year i got really into  playing tetris effect and while i was  playing it i had a strange but familiar  feeling it felt like i was totally in  the zone  hours went by in moments it was  somewhere between a sense of intense  focus and total relaxation despite how  intense tetris effect gets there was  something calming about playing it not  only that but afterwards it left me with  a long lasting satisfaction that i don't  usually get from playing games  in psychology terms this feeling is  known as flow we've all experienced this  sense of flow or feeling in the zone  while playing games and whether you  realize it or not that's probably when  you enjoy playing games the most and get  the most satisfaction from playing them  the idea of flow was defined by the work  of mihai chick sent me high the 86 year  old hungarian-american psychologist i  mentioned earlier his branch of  psychology is what's known as positive  psychology seeking to understand the  components to a happy life here he is  giving a ted talk about his research  called the secret to happiness i try to  understand now  where  in everyday life in our  normal experience  do we do we feel really happy since  understanding flow is supposedly the key  to happiness i got in touch with  alexander chris to help me get my head  around it he's a clinical psychologist  and author of the gaming mind a book  that looks at why we play games and the  positive impact that games can have on  the psychology of those who play them  flow is  the state of feeling totally immersed  in  a meaningful activity there's often a  sense of a subjective distortion of time  really feeling kind of lost in in the  activity suddenly realizing three hours  have passed and feeling kind of good  about what's that that's happened it's  usually accompanied by a kind of  pleasurable peaceful emotional state it  kind of paints a more organic peaceful  picture of what it means to sort of be  happy or sort of seek happiness chick  sent me high proposed that rather than  money or material possessions it was  through having more flow in their lives  that people found greater happiness  almost any activity can evoke flow but  those that do evoke flowing people tend  to exhibit some but not necessarily all  of these main characteristics that last  point that the activity slightly exceeds  our skill level seems to be a crucial  component  whenever you see writing on flow you  might spot a version of this graph  showing that flow occurs when both the  level of challenge and the skill  required for success are high  in his book finding flow chick sent me  high says games like chess tennis or  poker present easy opportunities for  flow states to occur because games have  goals and rules for action which make it  possible for the player to act without  questioning what should be done and how  it's easy to see that this can just as  easily be applied to many video games  are  activities that  seek to immerse players in a way that  really engages them and in some ways can  kind of take them out of the objectives  time and space outside of the game you  know in general this is this interactive  medium that involves a lot of ongoing  sometimes repetitive actions but but  also progressive actions right sort of  moving forward accomplishing things  getting better developing skills so all  these things potentially can be  meaningful to a player and therefore be  part of what induces a flow state the  interesting thing is that the games that  can put players into a state of flow  seem to vary wildly from person to  person and to prove this i got some of  my friends here at gamespot to talk  about the games that give them a feeling  of flow if there was one game that i can  guarantee would put me into a flow  almost trance-like state that game would  be doom  eternal mega man um dark souls  battlefield to an extent escape from  tacos  overwatch super monkey ball when it  comes to fighting games it's just you  and the other person and that's where  i've experienced being in the zone where  every combo is hitting every frame trap  is working out it is so intense to a  point where the game itself feels like a  blur it's all i can focus on it's all i  can think about and that carries you  forward and keeps you in the zone for  one of a better phrase so i'm completely  in the zone focusing on what's happening  you kind of feel like you sink into  yourself and sort of really feeling that  sort of relaxed vibe that the game has  that can easily just tip over into pure  terror all the action and shooting is  maintaining one portion of my brain  while the strategy is sort of occupying  the other like i'm turning my brain off  but i'm not i'm just so comfortable with  what i'm doing in the game that it's  just second nature at that point the  character is doing exactly just like  what you want it to do and the  controller and then the game is an  extension of yourself and it feels  amazing not think so much and just be in  the zone for what i'm doing my brain  really doesn't have space  for much else and i'm just rolling  around in the ball thinking about  rolling around in the ball you just  can't stop playing so how can it be that  games as varied as tetris effect super  monkey ball and doom eternal can produce  the same psychological effect  in different players well it won't  surprise you to know that many video  game developers have used research into  flow to help shape their games and maybe  the most obvious application of this  theory in a game  is flow  back in the mid-noughties genova chen  who you may know as the founder of that  game company and the director of journey  was a masters student at the university  of southern california's interactive  media and games division chen was  influenced by chick sent me high's  research and wanted to examine how games  could better maintain a player's sense  of flow because flow states require a  delicate balance of challenge and skill  and because players skill levels vary  greatly chen suggested that games could  offer subtle choices to the player to  allow them to essentially customize the  difficulty of the game in order to  subconsciously maintain a state of flow  chen referred to this as dynamic  difficulty adjustment and designed a  game called flow as part of his master's  thesis to demonstrate it flow offers  players a simplistic form of dynamic  difficulty as a primitive cellular life  form players feed on other organisms and  can through eating blue or red nodes  swim deeper to tackle bigger creatures  or back towards the surface to recover  the idea is that by factoring in a kind  of fluid difficulty adjustment within  the game's mechanics the player has  complete control over how challenging  the game is for them at any given moment  this was not a completely new idea to  games though it's not often completely  under the player's control resident evil  4 has a hidden dynamic difficulty  mechanic which will change the frequency  and behaviors of enemies dependent on  the player's skill and left 4 dead and  its sequel have an ai director who  dictates the rates at which certain  enemies weapons or health items spawn  depending on how well players are  performing less subtle examples also  exist mario kart's infamous rubber  banding system springs to mind however  many of these mechanics are designed to  prevent a game from becoming too easy or  too difficult for a player whereas chen  wanted to design a system to ensure  games sustain a sense of fun in his  thesis chen specifically outlines that  being in a flow state shares all the  characteristics of having fun the sense  of timelessness of being at one of  exhilaration focus and immediacy that if  a game evokes a flow experience it will  make the player lose track of time and  self-consciousness chen theorized that  if psychologists are studying the  science behind how to make people happy  it makes sense to apply that knowledge  when making games now whether or not you  think flow itself is successful at  maintaining a sense of fun the truth is  that genova chen is not the only  developer to figure this out in fact  plenty of modern games use a pursuit of  flow as a core tenet of their design in  an interview with noclip doom eternal's  game director hugo martin discusses in  detail the way the game offers challenge  and feedback to ensure players remain in  the fun zone while playing forcing them  to play in a certain style that requires  a flow-like mastery of the game's  mechanics in order to succeed  particularly on nightmare mode which  martin describes as the best version of  the game you know the fun zone is  managing resources it's doing all these  different things it's thinking  constantly it's using the right tool for  the job all these different things and  when they don't play that way  we kill them so is there a way to ensure  that every single time you sit down to  play a game you instantly enter a flow  state play for hours and leave feeling  satisfied  sadly it's probably not as simple as  that there might be something to the  idea that if you want to achieve flow  more often you should push yourself to  play more demanding games games whose  challenge slightly exceeds your skill  level as in the graph in reality however  it's very individual some may find flow  through completing chores in animal  crossing but find dark souls tedious as  chick sent me high himself says nobody  can tell you how to reach happiness  except yourself there's some degree of  mystery to this process still it's not  just a switch that flips on right where  you sit down and you're like enter this  state a lot of it has to do with the  players participation right they have to  really let themselves get into what  they're doing the thing that popped into  my mind is you know a few months ago i  was playing hades on the switch and  that's a game that has like no loading  times right and so which we should like  has to because it's so about dying and  being reborn like if it didn't move  quick it would stop being this sort of  constant movement so in that sweet spot  and it would become this sort of it does  it doesn't feel worth it and i think it  would rip you out of that state perhaps  a better idea is to find a game that you  enjoy that keeps you in the game as much  as possible and allows you to keep  playing for as long as possible  features like near instant loading times  and quick resume on newer consoles could  make this easier than ever many of the  games in which people experience flow  states while playing have an element of  never ending flow to their gameplay and  a constantly adapting goal be it newly  opened areas to explore are high score  to beat or multiplayer games that offer  thousands of new opponents to compete  with tetris is an obvious example of a  never-ending game in her book reality is  broken writer and game developer jane  mcgonagall sums it up perfectly saying  what makes tetris so addictive despite  the impossibility of winning is the  intensity of the feedback it provides it  only gets harder when you're playing  well creating a perfect balance between  hard challenge and achievability many  gamers would rather keep playing than  win thereby ending the game in high  feedback games the state of being  intensely engaged may ultimately be more  pleasurable than even the satisfaction  of winning the standout  example or series for me is the  civilization series right which i've  always been a fan of famously when you  win civilization it shows you the  victory screen and then there's a little  button that says just one more turn  right there like there is this awareness  that even though like the game is over  maybe you just kind of want to keep  going with it right this this sense that  that actually really is so much of the  experience it is not just the outcome of  victory but the the flow of play perhaps  this is why we love video games so much  they offer a potentially never ending  resource of a state of being that some  very clever people believe is the key to  a happy life it's enough to set alarm  bells ringing for reactionary  technophobe parents the world over the  bottom line is that according to chick  sent me high at least the more flow  people have in their lives the happier  their lives will be  chances are you already know the games  that give you that feeling but if not  you'll have to discover for yourself  what games can put you in a state of  flow let me know in the comments down  below what they are thanks again to  alexander chris for his involvement in  this video i've left a link to his book  and all the other books and videos that  i looked at during my research in the  description below you can also see a  video that i made a few months back  about dark souls and mental health in  the meantime leave a like and hit  subscribe if you've enjoyed the video so  that we can keep making content like  this thanks for watching  [Music]  [Music]  you

